  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/...\nHigh pressure will peak over the region on Wednesday, with \ntemperatures reaching about 10-15 degrees above normal and near \ndaily record highs. Warm and dry conditions will continue \nthrough early Saturday, with little change day to day from \nWednesday through Friday. High pressure will shift east by late \nFriday as southwest flow takes over ahead of a shift to cooler \nand wetter weather. An atmospheric river will impact the Pacific\nNorthwest on Friday and into the weekend, with the upper level \ntrough eventually moving east and bringing remnant moisture into\nsoutheast Oregon and southwest Idaho. Models diverge on when \nthe trough will move into the region, however all favor a cool \nand wet pattern for Sunday into early next week. This system \nwill also bring breezy southerly winds and a steady cooling \ntrend beginning Sunday. Precipitation chances increase to 20-40%\nchance on Saturday in high terrain in northeast Oregon and the \nWest Central Mountains in Idaho. Valley locations look to stay \nmostly dry through Saturday evening. Precipitation chances \nincrease area wide by Sunday, with a 20-40% chance in valleys, \nand 40-60% in higher elevations north and west of the Snake \nRiver Valley. Warm southwest flow ahead of the trough passage \nwill likely keep snow levels above 6500-7500 ft until they drop \nwith the colder air mass moving into the region early next week.",
